✅ QUESTION 2: FLOYD–WARSHALL ALGORITHM

(All-Pairs Shortest Path)

This one is a classic graph DP problem and very frequently asked in interviews + exams.

📌 Problem Statement

You are given a weighted directed graph with V vertices.
Some edges may have negative weights, but no negative cycles.

Your task is to find the shortest distance between every pair of vertices.

👉 If there is no path between two vertices, the distance should remain infinity (INF).

💡 Key Idea

Floyd–Warshall uses Dynamic Programming

It progressively improves shortest paths by allowing intermediate vertices

Works for:

Directed graphs

Negative weights

Detecting negative cycles

🧠 Core Concept

Let:

dist[i][j] = shortest distance from i to j


For every node k, check:

Can path i → k → j be shorter than i → j ?


So:

dist[i][j] = min(dist[i][j], dist[i][k] + dist[k][j])

🔁 Algorithm Steps

Initialize distance matrix dist

dist[i][j] = weight(i → j)

dist[i][i] = 0

For every intermediate node k

For every pair (i, j)

Relax the distance using k

After all iterations, dist[i][j] contains shortest paths

⏱ Time & Space Complexity

Time: O(V³)

Space: O(V²)

⚠️ Suitable only when V ≤ 500

☕ Java Code (Floyd–Warshall)
public class FloydWarshall {

    static final int INF = 99999;

    public static void floydWarshall(int[][] graph, int V) {

        int[][] dist = new int[V][V];

        // Step 1: Initialize distance matrix
        for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < V; j++) {
                dist[i][j] = graph[i][j];
            }
        }

        // Step 2: Pick intermediate vertices
        for (int k = 0; k < V; k++) {
            for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {
                for (int j = 0; j < V; j++) {

                    // Relaxation step
                    if (dist[i][k] != INF && dist[k][j] != INF &&
                        dist[i][k] + dist[k][j] < dist[i][j]) {

                        dist[i][j] = dist[i][k] + dist[k][j];
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        // Print final distance matrix
        printSolution(dist, V);
    }

    static void printSolution(int[][] dist, int V) {
        System.out.println("Shortest distances between every pair:");
        for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < V; j++) {
                if (dist[i][j] == INF)
                    System.out.print("INF ");
                else
                    System.out.print(dist[i][j] + " ");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int V = 4;

        int[][] graph = {
            {0,   5,  INF, 10},
            {INF, 0,   3, INF},
            {INF, INF, 0,  1},
            {INF, INF, INF, 0}
        };

        floydWarshall(graph, V);
    }
}

🧩 Line-by-Line Explanation
🔹 INF Constant
static final int INF = 99999;


Represents no direct edge between nodes.

🔹 Initialization
dist[i][j] = graph[i][j];


Initial distances are directly from the adjacency matrix.

🔹 Triple Loop (Heart of Algorithm)
for (int k = 0; k < V; k++)
    for (int i = 0; i < V; i++)
        for (int j = 0; j < V; j++)


k → allowed intermediate node

i → source

j → destination

🔹 Relaxation Check
if (dist[i][k] + dist[k][j] < dist[i][j])
    dist[i][j] = dist[i][k] + dist[k][j];


Checks if going via k gives a shorter path.

